speechtext:
That, in the opinion of this House, the Government should express concern about the continuing illegal Turkish colonization of Cyprus and the latest secessionist movement of the Denktash regime
1.	by supporting measures for the implementation of the United Nations resolutions on Cyprus;
2.	by ceasing further negotiations with the Turkish Government on the sale of a nuclear reactor, until the latter complies with the U.N. resolutions on Cyprus and shows a respect for international law;
3.	by calling upon the Turkish Government to provide immediate information on missing Cypriots;
4.	by calling upon the Turkish Government to respect the human rights provisions of the Helsinki accord, especially in regards to Cyprus;
5.	by calling upon the Turkish Government to cease immediately, further colonization of Cyprus and to withdraw all illegal colonists transplanted with a view to changing the demographic nature of the Island; and
6.	by condemning the new secessionist movement of the so-called "Federated State of Northern Cyprus".
He said: Mr. Speaker, I am pleased to be given an opportunity to present this motion to the House in order to give the House the opportunity of considering the request that 1 put forward in a motion.

Thank you very much, Mr. Speaker. The idea of putting this motion arose out of a function I attended in July of this year. It was a function organized by the Cypriot Federation of Canada along with the Hellenic-Canadian Federation of Ontario, the Cypriot Community of Toronto and the Greek Community of Metropolitan Toronto. This function was entitled "Justice for Cyprus". It was attended by Hon. Members from all sides of the House including my good friend, the Hon. Member for York East (Mr. Redway), and others. I do not want to take too long making my comments because I understand that many other Hon. Members want to say a few words about this motion. I would like to give them the opportunity to do so.
The question of Cyprus and its invasion by Turkey has been raised in the House several times by Hon. Members on all sides of the House, including the Hon. Member for Broadview-Greenwood (Ms. McDonald) and the Hon. Member for Brant (Mr. Blackburn.). It is well known that there have been many communal differences in Cyprus, some of which are of a long-standing nature. Nevertheless, there has also been a considerable period of time during which the two communities

December 17, 1985
Cyprus
have lived together fairly peacefully side by side. I am convinced that given a chance, those two communities would do so once again but only once the Turkish troops get off the island.
At the function to which I referred, all the participants made it a point that prior to the invasion, even though there were differences between the peoples of Cyprus, more often than not through interpersonal relationships they had got along very well over the years. They were quite convinced that they could do so again provided they were given the opportunity to make their own decisions on their own behalf without a strong military presence on the island.
This is the eleventh year since the Turkish invasion of Cyprus; 11 years since Turkey sent its army to occupy 40 per cent of Cyprus and displace one-third of its population or some 200,000 persons. Turkey also captured thousands of defenceless civilians and shot, butchered or raped hundreds and 2,000 of these are still missing. Turkey then brought in a flood of colonists from mainland Turkey to whom the stolen properties of the displaced refugees were handed over. Despite worldwide condemnation of this action by the United Nations General Assembly, the European Common Market and the Commonwealth, Turkey has ignored all resolutions and pleas for justice in Cyprus and has continued to support the Turkish pseudo state which Turkey alone recognizes.
Justice for Cyprus means the removal of Turkish troops and Turkish colonists from Cyprus, the return of the 200,000 refugees, an accounting of the 2,000 missing Cypriots and their release from Turkish jails. It means a free, united Cyprus as stated in UN Resolution 37/253 which was adopted in 1983.
If we are to achieve justice in Cyprus, we must have a clear understanding of who the aggressors and the victims are. This is not a problem between Greek and Turkish Cypriots and it is not an insoluble problem as the Turkish Government would like to portray it. It is nothing but the occupation by an aggressor of a small island country with a limited defence.
At every appropriate occasion, the Canadian Government at the UN and during Commonwealth discussions on the Middle East and Cyprus should raise the issue of Cyprus with other countries and particularly with NATO and the United States. There can be justice for Cyprus. The problem can be solved but only through outside mediation and influence, particularly through the United States. If the Canadian Government has as close a relationship with the United States administration as it says it has, and if Canada has a direct interest in Cyprus, Canada can indeed bring about a just solution for Cyprus and all of its citizens, both Greek Cypriots and Turkish Cypriots.
Although the island was invaded 11 years ago, during all this time the Government of Turkey and the Turkish Cypriots have ignored several resolutions put by the United Nations and the European Parliament which call for a just settlement. Turkey is also waging a campaign of intimidation against Greece and threatens the integrity of several Greek islands along the western coast. The continuation of Turkish aggression contemplates a threat to peace in the part of the world and goes against the collective interests of the western alliance.
It is because of the global concern over what has been occurring on Cyprus that the UN General Assembly adopted on May 13, 1983, Resolution 37/253. The vote on that resolution was 103 in favour with five against and 20 abstentions. I believe it is important that that resolution be recorded here. Therefore, I intend to make the House aware of it. The General Assembly Resolution reads as follows:
Having considered the question of Cyprus,
Recalling its resolution 3212 (XXIX) of 1 November 1974 and its subsequent resolutions on the question of Cyprus,
Recalling the high-level agreements of 12 February 1977 and 19 May 1979 Reaffirming the principle of the inadmissibility of occupation and acquisition of territories by force,
Greatly concerned at the prolongation of the Cyprus crisis, which poses a serious threat to international peace and security,
Deeply regretting that the resolutions of the United Nations on Cyprus have not yet been implemented,
Recalling the idea of holding an international conference on Cyprus,
Deploring the fact that part of the territory of the Republic of Cyprus is still occupied by foreign forces,
Deploring the lack of progress in the intercommunal talks,
Deploring all unilateral actions that change the demographic structure of Cyprus or promote faits accomplis,
Reaffirming the need to settle the question of Cyprus without further delay by peaceful means in accordance with the provisions of the Charter of the United Nations and the relevant United Nations resolutions,
1.	Reiterates its full support for the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity, unity and non-alignment of the Republic of Cyprus and calls once again for the cessation of all foreign interference in its affairs;
2.	Affirms the right of the Republic of Cyprus and its people to full and effective sovereignty and control over the entire territory of Cyprus and its natural and other resources and calls upon all States to support and help the Government of the Republic of Cyprus to exercise these rights;
3.	Condemns any act which tends to undermine the full and effective exercise of the above-mentioned rights, including the unlawful issue of titles of ownership of property;
4.	Welcomes the proposal for total demilitarization made by the President of the Republic of Cyprus;
5.	Express its support for the high-level agreements of 12 February 1977 and 19 May 1979 and all the provision thereof;
6.	Demands the immediate and effective implementation of resolution 3212 (XXIX), unanimously adopted by the General Assembly and endorsed by the Security Council in its resolution 365 (1974) of 13 December 1974, and of the subsequent resolutions of the Assembly and the Council on Cyprus which provide the valid and essential basis for the solution of the problem of Cyprus;
7.	Considers the withdrawal of all occupation forces from the Republic of Cyprus as an essential basis for a speedy and mutually acceptable solution of the Cyprus problem;
8.	Demands the immediate withdrawal of all occupation forces from the Republic of Cyprus;
9.	Commends the intensification of the efforts made by the Secretary-General, while noting with concern the lack of progress in the intercommunal talks;
10.	Calls for meaningful, result-oriented, constructive and substantive negotiations between the representatives of the two communities, under the auspices of the Secretary-General, to be conducted freely and on an equal footing, on the basis of relevant United Nations resolutions and the high-level agreements, with a view to reaching as early as possible a mutually acceptable agreement based on the fundamental and legitimate rights of the two communities;
11.	Calls for respect of the human rights and fundamental freedoms of all Cypriots, including the freedom of movement, the freedom of settlement and
December 17, 1985

the right to property and the instituting of urgent measures for the voluntary return of the refugees to their homes in safety;
12.	Considers that the de facto situation created by the force of arms should not be allowed to influence or in any way affect the solution of the problem of Cyprus;
13.	Calls upon the parties concerned to refrain from any unilateral action which might adversely affect the prospects of a just and lasting solution of the problem of Cyprus by peaceful means and to co-operate fully with the Secretary-General in the performance of his task under the relevant resolutions of the General Assembly and the Security Council as well as with the United Nations Peace-Keeping Force in Cyprus;
14.	Calls upon the parties concerned to refrain from any action which violates or is designated to violate the independence, unity, sovereignty and territorial integrity of the Republic of Cyprus;
15.	Reiterates its recommendation that the Security Council should examine the question of implementation, within a specified time-frame, of its relevant resolutions and consider and adopt thereafter, if necessary, all appropriate and practical measures under the Charter of the United Nations for ensuring the speedy and effective implementation of the resolutions of the United Nations on Cyprus;
16.	Welcomes the intention of the Secretary-General, as expressed in his report... to pursue a renewed personal involvement in the quest for a solution of the Cyprus problem and, in view of this, requests the Secretary-General to undertake such actions or initiatives as he may consider appropriate within the framework of the mission of good offices entrusted to him by the Security Council for promoting a just and lasting solution of the problem and to report to the General Assembly at its thirty-eighth session on the results of his efforts;
17.	Decides to include in the provisional agenda of its thirty-eighth session the item entitled "Question of Cyprus" and requests the Secretary-General to follow up the implementation of the present resolution and to report on all its aspects to the General Assembly at that session.
Some 86 voted in favour of this resolution, eight against and 25 abstained.
Hon. Members may be interested to know that on October 1 of this year the United States Senate adopted a similar resolution on Cyprus. The Senate resolved the following:
(a)	The Senate hereby congratulates the people of Cyprus on the twenty-fifth anniversary of their independence.
(b)	It is the sense of the Senate that the United States reaffirms its continuing commitment to a just resolution of the Cyprus dispute, and its support for the efforts of the Secretary-General to bring peace to that troubled nation.
At the outset I said that I did not have any intention of taking too long since other Members wish to speak on this matter. What I do say is that the arguments presented by the United Nations General Assembly are much more eloquent than ones I could put forward to the House. This is why it is important that I read the resolution into the record.
Also, it seems to me that as a country we have substantial influence and respect in international circles. That respect goes hand-in-hand with a high degree of credibility in the international arena. It seems to me that we should be using that credibility to use whatever influence we have on other nations to bring about a peaceful resolution of the problems in Cyprus. The number one call is for the withdrawal of Turkish troops from the island so that Cyprus itself can get on with making the decisions which will affect the lives of its people and future generations.
